WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.167
-Our next guest is the
legendary Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:04.734
stand-up comedian and actor.

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:07.634
You can see her performing
Charles Dickens'

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:10.767
"Great Expectations" at the
Greenwich Theater in New York.

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:13.634
Please welcome back or friend,
Eddie Izzard, everyone.

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:22.400
♪♪

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.167
♪♪

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:30.868
Welcome back, Eddie!

00:00:30.868 --> 00:00:32.300
-Very goodsies.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.834
I know I was here before.

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:38.200
In the -- one of the
dinosaur eras sometime ago.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.501
-Yeah, it's been a while --

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:40.634
-I like to space them out,

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:41.801
Just so you know,
it's nothing personal.

00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:43.100
-You don't like to do
them too close together.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.834
-I know I could have been back
earlier but I said,

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:48.567
No, let's make it long
so everyone says,

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.067
well, who? Who is this?

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.667
But it was back in one of the
dinosaur eras, and it was good.

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.501
You had a great show then,
and it's a great show now.

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:55.767
-Well, I'm very happy
to have you here.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:58.534
-I'm always a little
taken aback by

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:01.467
the challenges you present
to yourself as a performer.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:04.300
Obviously I know you as
a very accomplished stand-up,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.534
and yet here you are doing a
one-person show based on

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.801
Charles Dickens' "Great
Expectations."

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.300
Is this something
you always wanted to do?

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.501
-When I was 7 I wanted
to be an actor.

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:16.734
I don't know how comedy
attracted you

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:19.167
when you were a kid, but it was
just acting, just acting.

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:20.667
My mom died when I was a kid,
and I thought

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:21.901
Oh, the applause of the
audience,

00:01:21.901 --> 00:01:23.300
I kind of craved that love.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.067
And then -- I was a smaller kid.

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:29.133
I was dyslexic so I couldn't do
sight readings for auditions,

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:31.434
so I was the jailer or the
butler, or the thing.

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:32.734
Not the interesting one.

00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:34.701
I was once asked, what kind of
role would you like?

00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:37.100
And I said, well, you know those
big roles with the sword

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.634
and you kill everyone and you
get the girl?

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.033
That kind of role.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.267
And they said,
what about jailer?

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:43.767
[ Groans ]

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.701
I didn't know why I was being
asked about it,

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:48.200
'cause, really,
everybody wants the big role.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.267
But now, I -- This is the
punishment for my own ego.

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:53.701
I do every single one --
15,000 words.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:56.267
I'm playing everyone, if people
know "Great Expectations,"

00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:59.434
from Pip to Magwitch,
The Convict, to Estella,

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.033
Miss Havisham,
I'm playing them all.

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:04.334
And, so, I can only complain to
myself.

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:05.334
But I love it. I do love it.

00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:06.501
And it is dramatic.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:08.467
There are certain parts
which are comedic.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:12.267
And tomorrow is my birthday.

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.534
-Happy birthday!

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:15.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:21.033
-But, exciting, tomorrow is also
Charles Dickens' birthday.

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:22.167
-Well.
-Yes.

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:23.934
-Is this some sort of
cosmic --

00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:25.467
-It is a kind of cosmic.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.567
There's no word after cosmic.
Just a cosmic.

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:31.267
And it's a -- I knew this from
when I was a kid.

00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:33.067
But I did the audio book.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:35.067
I got someone to pay me to do
the audio book

00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:36.267
'cause I don't read,
I'm dyslexic,

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:37.567
so I was a very slow reader.

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:39.033
They paid me to read the book.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.834
So the audio book of "Great
expectations," 20 hours of it,

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.934
is out there, and you can
download it at your pleasure.

00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:46.400
But I thought let's do a stage
show to go with it

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.601
using the technique of
Richard Pryor.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.701
Which, Richard Pryor,
the great Richard Pryor,

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.567
in his stand-up he would
occasionally have characters

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.467
going, hey, what do you think?
[ Mumbles ]

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:56.334
And they would turn to
each other and talk.

00:02:56.334 --> 00:02:58.100
And that's what I did throughout
my stand-up

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.434
thinking that's what
everyone did.

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:01.200
A lot of stand-ups
don't do that, actually.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.534
-It's very hard.
You're one of my favorites.

00:03:03.534 --> 00:03:06.801
During that, I remember seeing
you live and realizing

00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:08.934
how you were in the
body of stand-up

00:03:08.934 --> 00:03:11.501
doing what seemed like sketch
comedy almost.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:13.000
You felt like it was a
visualization

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.000
of all these characters.
-And that's where I came from.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.534
I came from very Monty Python
influenced sketch comedy.

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:19.767
Just playing one character with
this kind of --

00:03:19.767 --> 00:03:21.167
And then playing another
character

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.868
with this kind of voice
in a different sketch.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:26.501
You now, and then some other
voice and different physicality.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.534
But now they're all together
talking to each other in,

00:03:28.534 --> 00:03:29.901
like, a weird chess game.

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:33.334
And in the Dickens, I sometimes
ad lib as Estella or Pip,

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:34.434
or Magwitch.

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:38.767
I make things up
in Dickensian speak.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.901
Which is little Easter eggs

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.067
for people who really
know the script.

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:43.567
No one could memorize
the script.

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:46.501
-Have you ever, you know,
ad libed outside of the text

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:48.400
and had someone
in the audience go, "Hey!"?

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.901
-No.

00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:52.000
'Cause I would go, "What?"

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.534
"You said the words wrong."

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:55.000
"Well, [ bleep ] you."

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.234
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.200
No, there's a line -- there's
one which I like,

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.234
which I'm not allowed to take
out because it's too funny.

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:02.834
Which is Uncle Pumblechook, this
kind of pompous guy,

00:04:02.834 --> 00:04:04.701
Uncle Pumblechook was a large,
hard-breathing man.

00:04:04.701 --> 00:04:06.267
Face like a fish,
dull, staring eyes

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:08.467
with hair that stuck up
like tuffs on his head.

00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:10.000
That's the actual description.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.033
And I changed it to large,
hard-breathing man,

00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:13.567
face like a fish,
dull, staring eyes,

00:04:13.567 --> 00:04:16.501
and hair that had obviously been
arranged by a dying relative.

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:17.868
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:19.400
Which kills every time.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.501
And I don't even lean into it.
I just throw it away.

00:04:22.501 --> 00:04:24.834
And I'm not allowed to change it
'cause it's too funny.

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:26.200
-Well, you know what?

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.133
I always thought somebody could
have done a punch-up pass

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:30.434
on Dickens, you know?

00:04:30.434 --> 00:04:33.767
Just add a few more jokes.
-Yeah, I know.

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:35.634
-He loves the great comic
description of stuff,

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:37.167
but he doesn't really go
for the line.

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:39.367
I'm sometimes leaning into his
lines to make them

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.400
pop out a bit more, but now I'm
actually taking that away

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.100
'cause it's the drama
that I love.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.968
You know, people think,
oh, yeah, you can do comedy.

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:47.367
But the dramatic ending...
Pip is desperate.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:49.133
He loves this girl, Estella,

00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:50.801
but she just doesn't
care for him.

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:52.868
And it never happens for him.
It's unrequited love.

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:54.067
I've been through that.

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:55.567
A lot of us in the world
have been through that.

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:57.467
Some of the very pretty people
don't go through that.

00:04:57.467 --> 00:04:58.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:02.200
But they're all bastards.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.033
-So you had your brother
actually adapted this.

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:09.300
And, you know,
this is sort of a bridging

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.601
of this very long, lengthy work.

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:13.601
Is this something,
a skill he had?

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:17.033
Was he surprised when you
came to him with this?

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:18.167
-He's probably
more creative than me.

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:19.534
He's a cartoonist and a writer.

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:20.934
He wrote a book
when he was in his 20s.

00:05:20.934 --> 00:05:22.734
And then,
my older brother, Mark,

00:05:22.734 --> 00:05:25.501
he decided that he
wasn't gonna go after that.

00:05:25.501 --> 00:05:29.467
And, so, I said to him on this,
I was trying to lure him in.

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:30.701
'Cause I know he's creative,
and I said,

00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:32.234
should we adapt it together?

00:05:32.234 --> 00:05:34.000
'Cause I was writing my own
scripts at this point.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.100
And he said, yeah, okay.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.801
And then he said,
I've done the first part.

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:38.334
There's three parts, I've done
the first part,

00:05:38.334 --> 00:05:39.601
which is quite a chunk of work.

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:40.901
And then he said, now I've
done the second part.

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:42.801
And, so,
I just let him go do it.

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:43.934
And now he's done "Hamlet."

00:05:43.934 --> 00:05:46.968
"Hamlet" is also on the --
in waiting.

00:05:46.968 --> 00:05:49.000
I'm gonna do
a one-woman Hamlet as well.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.234
-That's very exciting.
-So, uh, these are just there --

00:05:51.234 --> 00:05:53.100
And tomorrow on my birthday, I'm
doing an extra gig

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.467
at the JP Morgan's library.

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:57.767
John Pierpont Morgan,
the great --

00:05:57.767 --> 00:05:59.567
well, not the great --
I don't know, great is --

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:03.234
Conjecture there.
-Top five Morgans. Top five.

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:06.467
I'm not sure what he was like a
person, but he left his house.

00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:08.334
He didn't stay holding onto it
when he was dead.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:09.434
He let it go.

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:11.901
And I'm in his library tomorrow
night.

00:06:11.901 --> 00:06:14.567
After my show I'm doing
an extra show unplugged.

00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:17.067
Just me doing the verbals.
No lights and stuff.

00:06:17.067 --> 00:06:18.968
-Another thing you're
brother helped you out with

00:06:18.968 --> 00:06:20.334
that I was obviously
aware you did this.

00:06:20.334 --> 00:06:21.801
I did not realize
he was your translator.

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:24.801
Another thing that I've been in
awe of is that you will do

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:28.133
your stand-up act in
other languages.

00:06:28.133 --> 00:06:37.334
[ Speaking foreign languages ]

00:06:37.334 --> 00:06:40.300
Yeah, and encourage anyone,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.868
any stand-up,
particularly stand-ups

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:44.100
from all around the world --
Do it in a second language.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.434
It's tough,
but particularly in English.

00:06:45.434 --> 00:06:46.634
Anyone who's got another
language,

00:06:46.634 --> 00:06:48.434
if you do it in English,
you can tour the world.

00:06:48.434 --> 00:06:50.767
I have friends, French kid
called [ Inaudible ]

00:06:50.767 --> 00:06:52.968
and he played in
Helsinki in Finland.

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:55.133
And he was a French guy
talking in English.

00:06:55.133 --> 00:06:56.868
And the Finnish guys were
listening in English

00:06:56.868 --> 00:06:58.367
and laughing, and it works.

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:00.801
It's a bridging.
English is a bridging language.

00:07:00.801 --> 00:07:03.267
It's not our language --
British, English --

00:07:03.267 --> 00:07:05.667
It's not yours.
It's not the Australians.

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:07.133
It's not the
English-speaking Canadians.

00:07:07.133 --> 00:07:11.000
It is an open source language
just like the computer thing.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.701
Anyone can use English just --

00:07:12.701 --> 00:07:15.767
As we do around the world to
communicate business,

00:07:15.767 --> 00:07:17.067
to help medicine, you know.

00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:19.667
English is an easy language
to grab hold of.

00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:21.834
-With comedy, though, there must
be situations

00:07:21.834 --> 00:07:23.834
where you have to change --
-No.

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:26.033
-To do a direct translation
is working against you.

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:27.701
-No, it's only the references.

00:07:27.701 --> 00:07:30.667
Make sure you say KitKat.

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:34.100
'Cause KitKats are
around the world.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.834
You know,
if I hit them with a KitKat,

00:07:35.834 --> 00:07:38.634
then they're, oh a KitKat,
that's a little biscuit thing.

00:07:38.634 --> 00:07:40.734
But if you say with a Twinkie,
they go --

00:07:40.734 --> 00:07:43.767
Twinkies, I'm afraid,
didn't get outside --

00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:46.501
-Oh, so you have to find
universal products.

00:07:46.501 --> 00:07:47.834
-Yeah, universal comedy.

00:07:47.834 --> 00:07:50.434
But if you talk about human
sacrifice, everyone know.

00:07:50.434 --> 00:07:52.000
[ Laughter ]

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.501
You say, basically, human
sacrifice was,

00:07:56.501 --> 00:08:01.067
okay, the crops are bad,
the Gods obviously hate us,

00:08:01.067 --> 00:08:02.868
so we're gonna kill Steve.

00:08:02.868 --> 00:08:05.701
And then it will probably
pick up next year.

00:08:05.701 --> 00:08:07.868
And Steve is going, why me?

00:08:07.868 --> 00:08:09.934
Well, Steve,
I never did like you,

00:08:09.934 --> 00:08:12.033
and I'm the witch doctor with
the bone through the nose.

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:13.501
And that's how that works.

00:08:13.501 --> 00:08:16.434
You talk about human sacrifice,
they get in it Russia, in China,

00:08:16.434 --> 00:08:18.267
they get it in Istanbul.

00:08:18.267 --> 00:08:19.834
It doesn't matter where you are,

00:08:19.834 --> 00:08:22.834
people understand what you're
talking about, human sacrifice.

00:08:22.834 --> 00:08:24.567
It's just a thing that every
country understands

00:08:24.567 --> 00:08:26.634
that thousands of years ago,
we were all idiots,

00:08:26.634 --> 00:08:28.767
and all doing it.
Being controlled.

00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:31.767
-I mean, this is -- I do think
in general, you talk about,

00:08:31.767 --> 00:08:34.834
sorry, excuse me,
themes that are universal.

00:08:34.834 --> 00:08:38.534
Things like the universe,
religion, like humanity.

00:08:38.534 --> 00:08:40.501
Was that where you started
as stand-up?

00:08:40.501 --> 00:08:42.868
With the big ideas?
Or did you work your way there?

00:08:42.868 --> 00:08:44.968
-Well, you know, if you start
with the big ideas,

00:08:44.968 --> 00:08:46.868
People go, boring.
-Yeah.

00:08:46.868 --> 00:08:49.067
I actually have a theory of the
universe.

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.133
I've worked out how it works.

00:08:50.133 --> 00:08:53.000
And I could be wrong,
but I have --

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.501
It's not a comedy version
of the theory.

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:57.701
I was using it in my last show.

00:08:57.701 --> 00:08:59.501
It was basically that
everything is circular

00:08:59.501 --> 00:09:01.567
and the big bang, the
crunch, big bang, big crunch,

00:09:01.567 --> 00:09:03.234
No Gods,
no one's controlling it,

00:09:03.234 --> 00:09:04.701
we're just lucky enough
to be here.

00:09:04.701 --> 00:09:06.467
Meaning of life, there is no
meaning,

00:09:06.467 --> 00:09:08.000
but some of us put meaning
into life

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.801
and some of us take it
out of life.

00:09:09.801 --> 00:09:13.434
So, this was my thing, but there
was no jokes in it, initially.

00:09:13.434 --> 00:09:15.667
So I had to put in some jokes
along the way

00:09:15.667 --> 00:09:17.367
of me trying to say
this is my theory

00:09:17.367 --> 00:09:19.300
on how the universe works.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.667
-I think, in a very good way,
comedy has become a place where

00:09:22.667 --> 00:09:24.000
you can have a big idea,

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.434
and then if you make sure
to put jokes into it,

00:09:26.434 --> 00:09:30.033
people are willing to stand
around and be explained.

00:09:30.033 --> 00:09:31.801
They like having a few jokes
to go with it.

00:09:31.801 --> 00:09:33.300
-I call it comedy philosophy.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.868
Yeah, it's like we can be
comic philosophers if we wish.

00:09:35.868 --> 00:09:37.801
We can say, what is it?
How does it work?

00:09:37.801 --> 00:09:39.801
How do religions work?
Is one of them right?

00:09:39.801 --> 00:09:41.100
Is the other one right?

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.334
Is a god gonna come down and
say I'm the God Ping?

00:09:43.334 --> 00:09:46.400
And I believe that all soot
should be saluted?

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.267
That's it, you know?

00:09:48.267 --> 00:09:50.434
And you go, oh, I thought --
what about the other god?

00:09:50.434 --> 00:09:51.634
No, they're wrong.
It's just me.

00:09:51.634 --> 00:09:53.467
And then we go, what?

00:09:53.467 --> 00:09:55.400
You could talk about God 'cause
every religion

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:56.634
to the next religion
looks crazy.

00:09:56.634 --> 00:09:57.934
And I don't believe
in any of them.

00:09:57.934 --> 00:09:59.334
I just believe in human beings.

00:09:59.334 --> 00:10:01.067
-There you go, that's a pretty
good thing to believe in.

00:10:01.067 --> 00:10:03.601
-Human beings are here,
and I can get in with them.

00:10:03.601 --> 00:10:05.334
-Do you remember --

00:10:05.334 --> 00:10:08.000
What was your first,
what was your first show

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.300
in the states?

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:13.634
-First one was, I did the
Memphis in May festival in 1988.

00:10:13.634 --> 00:10:14.701
-In Memphis, Tennessee?

00:10:14.701 --> 00:10:16.133
-In Memphis Tennessee in
Overton square,

00:10:16.133 --> 00:10:18.567
If anyone's in Overton Square.
I actually went back there.

00:10:18.567 --> 00:10:20.634
I said I began here
and no one cared.

00:10:20.634 --> 00:10:23.634
I did my first show --
-No statue? No plaque?

00:10:23.634 --> 00:10:26.667
-I was building. Hammering in --

00:10:26.667 --> 00:10:28.300
Get that thing out of the way.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:30.968
It was a car park
and now it's [ Inaudible ]

00:10:30.968 --> 00:10:33.334
But it's a really good, anyway,
I did it there,

00:10:33.334 --> 00:10:34.868
and I said I could
go back to London.

00:10:34.868 --> 00:10:38.367
Maybe I can go via New York.
'cause obviously this thing

00:10:38.367 --> 00:10:40.400
of being in New York,
and I could worked it out,

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.534
I could pay them some money
and they said,

00:10:42.534 --> 00:10:44.801
Washington Square park is where
the street performers perform.

00:10:44.801 --> 00:10:46.501
I was a street performer for
four years

00:10:46.501 --> 00:10:48.901
and that gives me
a special, weird ability

00:10:48.901 --> 00:10:50.834
to perform out
when no one's expecting it.

00:10:50.834 --> 00:10:54.234
And in the fountain of
Washington Square Park,

00:10:54.234 --> 00:10:56.334
that's the first ever show
I did in 1988.

00:10:56.334 --> 00:10:58.934
I made about $20,
and I was pleased.

00:10:58.934 --> 00:11:02.200
-Well, 20 bucks in 1988,
that's, like, $28 now.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:05.167
-Exactly, and it's
really kicked up.

00:11:05.167 --> 00:11:07.534
But also, I could tell
everyone back in London

00:11:07.534 --> 00:11:10.567
I've performed in New York.

00:11:10.567 --> 00:11:12.601
No one ever sees you doing gigs.

00:11:12.601 --> 00:11:14.367
I was very funny, too.

00:11:14.367 --> 00:11:17.901
-Do you think that doing those
as a street performer,

00:11:17.901 --> 00:11:21.901
ultimately, any show you do now
is so much easier

00:11:21.901 --> 00:11:23.634
because you do have
people's attention?

00:11:23.634 --> 00:11:26.234
-Yes. Yes, it is.
But also they demand, you know?

00:11:26.234 --> 00:11:29.200
The audience, when you are on
the street, can say,

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.534
well, this isn't very good.
We're going.

00:11:30.534 --> 00:11:32.834
But they don't
throw things at you.

00:11:32.834 --> 00:11:34.767
Not that most audiences throw
things at you.

00:11:34.767 --> 00:11:38.634
But it's easier when
they're there.

00:11:38.634 --> 00:11:41.801
The French audience and the
German audience are very polite.

00:11:41.801 --> 00:11:43.667
And the Spanish was, actually.

00:11:43.667 --> 00:11:45.000
The British ones are not.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:51.400
[ Mimicking audience ]

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:52.667
You know, this kind of stuff.

00:11:52.667 --> 00:11:54.267
I don't know if that happens
in New York as well.

00:11:54.267 --> 00:11:55.834
-They call you a cab from stage?

00:11:55.834 --> 00:11:57.901
-Yeah. They say, "Cab for Eddie
Izzard," you know.

00:11:57.901 --> 00:12:00.467
That was the big joke.

00:12:00.467 --> 00:12:01.767
"Say something funny."

00:12:01.767 --> 00:12:03.934
I knew I was getting good when
somebody heckled me once

00:12:03.934 --> 00:12:06.601
and said, "say something funny,"
and I went, "No."

00:12:06.601 --> 00:12:08.100
And then just carried on.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:11.234
And I go, whoa, okay,
my confidence is high there.

00:12:11.234 --> 00:12:14.767
I used to do these elaborate
putdowns and heckles.

00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:17.534
Someone heckles,
I'd say, this guy is Kenny.

00:12:17.534 --> 00:12:19.267
He's having a lot of
problems at the moment.

00:12:19.267 --> 00:12:21.601
His doctor said come along
and shout at me.

00:12:21.601 --> 00:12:22.467
Well done, Kenny.

00:12:22.467 --> 00:12:24.667
You shouted out, "Go away,"

00:12:24.667 --> 00:12:26.100
but used more of
the swear words there.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:29.901
And make it into a sentence,
but keep going.

00:12:29.901 --> 00:12:32.067
In five minutes he will be
heckling again.

00:12:32.067 --> 00:12:34.300
Well done, Kenny.
We're with you.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:36.534
And you impose scenarios on him.

00:12:36.534 --> 00:12:39.701
Any stand-up out there can use
this, well, yeah, use it.

00:12:39.701 --> 00:12:41.801
It's a technique,
just impose scenarios.

00:12:41.801 --> 00:12:44.567
And then everyone thinks, they
know it's not Kenny, but

00:12:44.567 --> 00:12:46.234
they go, "It's Kenny again."

00:12:46.234 --> 00:12:48.367
"What was that?
That wasn't even a sentence.

00:12:48.367 --> 00:12:49.634
You're going backwards, Kenny."

00:12:49.634 --> 00:12:51.801
It's a lovely little
technique to use

00:12:51.801 --> 00:12:53.334
when people are
shouting things out.

00:12:53.334 --> 00:12:55.234
-Well, it is so lovely
to have you here.

00:12:55.234 --> 00:12:58.100
Thank you so much,
and always a pleasure.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:12:59.367
That's Eddie Izzard, everybody.

00:12:59.367 --> 00:13:01.734
"Great Expectations"
playing at

00:13:01.734 --> 00:13:03.501
The Greenwich Theater
through February 11th.

00:13:03.501 --> 00:13:04.968
We'll be right back with
more "Late Night."

